// A function's summary: bit i is set when its i-th parameter may be kept past
// the call -- stored in a list, returned, exported, aliased, or passed on to
// something that keeps it. A clear bit is a proof that the caller may free the
// list it passed once the call returns. Bit 15 + i is the second mask (see
// esc_dbit): the body may read an element of that parameter out into a keeping
// position or store a non-literal element into it. Bit 30 says the function
// may return something that is not a block it made itself (fresh/ret.id): a
// clear bit is a proof that the caller owns what it gets back. -1 is every
// bit, the answer for anything unknown, and every bit set is the cautious
// answer for all three.
//
// Computed on demand and remembered in esc_pe, one function at a time, by
// scc/: a function that calls itself, directly or through others, is computed
// by repeating its body from "nothing kept, everything fresh" until no mask
// grows, which is the least fixpoint and is sound because every bit only ever
// turns on. While it runs, a call that reaches back to it reads its mask so
// far.

esc_mask(string cname) {
  int m = 0 - 1;
  int ix = fix_fnix(cname);
  if(ix >= 0) {
    m = esc_mask_at(ix, cname);
  }
} return int m;
